user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a white box in the center containing a list of options for identity verification. The title of the box is \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n\n* The CoreID Admin Console is visible on the left side of the image.\n\t+ It has a dark blue background with white text.\n\t+ The menu options are listed vertically, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n* The white box in the center of the image contains a list of options for identity verification.\n\t+ The title of the box is \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n\t+ There are two options listed: \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n\t+ Both options have a checkbox next to them, indicating that they are selected.\n\t+ Below the options, there is a message that reads \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n\nThe image suggests that the user is attempting to verify their identity by matching their employee ID and manager's name. The fact that two out of four options are confirmed indicates that the user has already provided some information, but still needs to provide more to complete the verification process. Overall, the image appears to be a screenshot of a secure login or verification process, where the user is required to provide additional information to access their account or perform a specific action.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a password reset page for an employee named J. Employee, with a temporary password provided and a button to generate a new one.\n\n* The page is titled \"Reset Password - J. Employee\" and has a white background with black text.\n\t+ The title is centered at the top of the page.\n\t+ Below the title, there is a section with the temporary password and a checkbox to force a change at the next sign-in.\n\t+ There is a blue button labeled \"Generate Temporary Password\" below the checkbox.\n* The page has a navigation menu on the left side with the following options:\n\t+ CoreID\n\t+ Admin Console\n\t+ Dashboard\n\t+ Tickets\n\t+ Users\n\t+ Reports\n\t+ Settings\n* The page also has a header with the user's name and a link to sign out.\n\nThe page appears to be a standard password reset page, with a clear and concise layout. The temporary password is provided, and the user can choose to force a change at the next sign-in. The navigation menu provides access to other features and settings. Overall, the page is well-designed and easy to use. \n\nThere are no errors or warnings visible on the page. The page is likely used for employees to reset their passwords, and the temporary password is provided for security purposes. The page is well-designed and easy to use, making it a good user experience. \n\nThe likely issue category is Software/Application, with a severity of Medium. The reason is that the page is a standard password reset page, but it may not be suitable for all users or situations. The page is well-designed, but it may not be accessible to all users with disabilities. The page is also not secure, as the temporary password is visible and can be easily accessed by others. \n\nThe observed facts are:\n\n* The page is titled \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n* The page has a white background with blac